# <%= current_page.data.title %>

**Notice:** This content was AI assisted. Get in touch with the author if you're unsure.

This guide explains how ASO manages PostgreSQL through Flux and how a team
updates its engine version in its existing patch while inheriting the shared
resource and API. The examples below follow Adoption and RPE preview in CFT. As
this is largly utilised in preview, we will be glossing over downtime/CR and
precautions which
would naturally be required if executing on prod. Prod postgres servers are not
built with Flux so these instructions are not valid (please see the
[Terraform instructions](create-modes-in-postgres-flexible-server.html)).

**Development teams:** [jump to Quick upgrade](#quick-upgrade) for the example
change, screenshots and commands. The background and full implementation
guidance follow below.

## Prerequisites

- Access to the relevant Flux repository and the correct Azure server.
- Platform Operations confirm that the target cluster's ASO release and
  CRDs support the selected resource API and intended upgrade.
- A supported PostgreSQL upgrade path, application and extension compatibility
  checks, and a non-production test before rollout.

<%= warning_text('A PostgreSQL major-version upgrade causes downtime and affects every database on the server. Follow the Change Request process for production. A completed upgrade cannot be reversed by reverting the pull request or lowering the version in Git.') %>

