## Understanding ASO and shared resources (theory)

### From Flux to Azure

Flux reconciles repository configuration into each AKS cluster. Azure Service
Operator (ASO) runs in the cluster and uses Kubernetes custom resources to manage
Azure infrastructure. A `FlexibleServer` resource describes an Azure PostgreSQL
server; the database itself runs in Azure, not in an AKS pod.

```text
Flux repository
  -> Kustomize combines shared resources and team patches
  -> Flux substitutes environment values and applies the manifests to AKS
  -> ASO reconciles the resources into Azure
```

CFT uses [hmcts/cnp-flux-config](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config);
SDS uses [hmcts/sds-flux-config](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-flux-config).
The examples here are CFT paths relative to the repository root. Check the
equivalent configuration before applying this pattern in SDS.

### Three different versions

| Setting | What it controls |
| --- | --- |
| ASO release, for example `v2.17.0` | The controller and CRDs available in the cluster, including supported resource APIs. |
| `apiVersion`, for example `dbforpostgresql.azure.com/v20250801` | The resource schema and Azure management API used by ASO. This example corresponds to Azure API `2025-08-01`. |
| `spec.version`, for example `"17"` | The requested PostgreSQL engine major version. |

An ASO or API bump alone does not select a newer PostgreSQL engine.
Changing `spec.version` on an existing server is a database upgrade request.

Platform Operations manages ASO compatibility and shared defaults. Development
teams manage their server overrides and verify application compatibility.
The ASO release is configured in
[apps/azureserviceoperator-system/aso/kustomization.yaml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/azureserviceoperator-system/aso/kustomization.yaml);
confirm the deployed controller and CRDs are ready before using a new API.

### Shared template, team-specific server

The shared PostgreSQL manifest is a reusable Kustomize resource, not a single
database shared by all teams. Each consuming application renders its own server.
Team patches override only the fields they specify; all other settings are
inherited.

| Layer | Example | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shared server | [apps/azureserviceoperator-system/resources/flexibleserver-postgres.yaml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/azureserviceoperator-system/resources/flexibleserver-postgres.yaml) | Defaults for API, engine, SKU, storage, networking and ownership. |
| Team patch | [apps/adoption/preview/aso/adoption-postgres.yaml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/adoption/preview/aso/adoption-postgres.yaml) | Matches the shared server API and sets team-specific overrides, including the engine version. |
| Environment Kustomization | [apps/adoption/preview/base/kustomization.yaml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/adoption/preview/base/kustomization.yaml) | Imports shared resources and applies the existing team patch. |

The shared server and team patches use
`dbforpostgresql.azure.com/v20250801`. The team patch overrides the inherited
engine version for that team's server. For example, Adoption's patch currently
specifies `"14"`; a planned upgrade changes that existing field to the approved
target, such as `"17"`. The API version remains aligned with the shared
resource.

The shared engine default is currently `"17"`, independently of its 2025 API.
Do not confuse an API year with an engine version. A consumer without an engine
override inherits the shared default, so changing shared defaults can affect
other teams and existing servers.

## How-to steps

### 1. Identify the server and upgrade scope

Follow the application's Flux path to its environment Kustomization. RPE uses
[apps/rpe/base/kustomize.yaml](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/apps/rpe/base/kustomize.yaml)
to select `apps/rpe/${ENVIRONMENT}/base`.

Confirm the current engine version in Azure or through a database connection:

```sql
SHOW server_version;
```

Compare the running version with the existing team patch and shared defaults.
Review Microsoft's [major-version upgrade guidance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/postgresql/flexible-server/concepts-major-version-upgrade)
for supported targets and pre-upgrade requirements.


### 2. Update the version in the existing team patch

Edit the existing team patch referenced by the environment Kustomization. Keep
its `apiVersion`, `kind` and resource identity aligned with the shared server.
Change only `spec.version` for an individual team's engine upgrade:

```yaml
apiVersion: dbforpostgresql.azure.com/v20250801
kind: FlexibleServer
metadata:
  name: ${NAMESPACE}-${ENVIRONMENT}
  namespace: ${NAMESPACE}
spec:
  version: "17"
```
 
Confirm that the environment Kustomization already references the team patch, and that the chosen engine
version is supported. (v2025 supports up to postgresql v18)

### 3. Validate and roll out

From the Flux repository root, lint the changed team patch and render its
environment overlay. For Adoption preview:

```bash
yamllint -c .yamllint.yaml \
  apps/adoption/preview/base/kustomization.yaml \
  apps/adoption/preview/aso/adoption-postgres.yaml

kubectl kustomize apps/adoption/preview/base --load-restrictor LoadRestrictionsNone
```

The repository-wide lint command is `./tests/lint-yaml.sh`. CI also runs
Flux dry-runs and schema validation through
[tests/dry-run-kustomize.sh](https://github.com/hmcts/cnp-flux-config/blob/master/tests/dry-run-kustomize.sh).

Check the rendered server has:

- The existing API `dbforpostgresql.azure.com/v20250801` and the intended
  `spec.version`.
- The original name, namespace, owner, networking and other server settings.
- No unintended changes to other servers or server configuration resources.

A local Kustomize build does not perform Flux substitutions or prove a
successful Azure upgrade. Confirm the rendered version and CI results as well.

Raise a pull request. 
Merge an environment's version change only when ready for Flux and ASO to
reconcile it.

### 4. Monitor the Flux and ASO upgrade
(If you have arrived here from the Quickstart, replace 'rpe' with your app)

Check that the current context is the intended cluster, then confirm the RPE
Kustomization is ready and see which source revision it applied:

```bash
kubectl config current-context
flux get kustomizations -A | grep rpe
kubectl get kustomization rpe -n flux-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.path}{"\n"}{.status.lastAppliedRevision}{"\n"}'
```

If the application is deployed through Helm, check its HelmReleases as well.
This is separate from checking whether ASO has completed the database upgrade.

```bash
flux get helmreleases -A
```

List the PostgreSQL resources in the namespace and describe the server:

```bash
kubectl get flexibleservers -n rpe
kubectl describe flexibleserver rpe-preview -n rpe
```

For the server's desired and Azure-reported versions and reconciliation state,
use query

```bash
kubectl get flexibleserver rpe-preview -n rpe \
  -o jsonpath='API: {.apiVersion}{"\n"}Desired version: {.spec.version}{"\n"}Azure-reported version: {.status.version}{"\n"}Ready: {.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].status}{"\n"}Reason: {.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].reason}{"\n"}Message: {.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].message}{"\n"}Generation: {.metadata.generation}{"\n"}Observed generation: {.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Ready")].observedGeneration}{"\n"}'
```

To save the full resource for local troubleshooting, use either command:

```bash
kubectl get flexibleserver rpe-preview -n rpe -o yaml
kubectl describe flexibleserver rpe-preview -n rpe > out.txt
```

While the upgrade runs, the desired version may be `"17"` while the
Azure-reported `status.version` remains `"14"`. A `Ready=False` condition with
reason `Reconciling` and a message that the resource is being reconciled means
ASO is still processing the Azure operation. Wait and check again; do not start
a second upgrade. Completion is indicated when the resource is Ready and the
reported version is `"17"`. Confirm the database version with
`SHOW server_version;` as well.

If reconciliation reports failure, inspect the Kustomization and ASO controller
events/logs before taking action:

```bash
kubectl describe kustomization rpe -n flux-system
kubectl logs -n azureserviceoperator-system \
  deploy/azureserviceoperator-controller-manager --since=30m \
  | grep -i rpe-preview
```

Avoid sharing a full `FlexibleServer` YAML dump outside approved channels: the
`serviceoperator.azure.com/poller-resume-token` annotation can contain
long-lived-looking Azure operation URLs and signed poller data. Prefer the
focused JSONPath command above, which omits that annotation. Full YAML and
`kubectl describe` output may include it; inspect saved output locally and redact
the annotation before sharing.

## Verification

- Flux and ASO reconcile successfully, with no outstanding server error.
- Azure reports the expected server ready on the target engine version.
- `SHOW server_version;` confirms the running PostgreSQL major version.
- The endpoint is unchanged and expected databases and data are present.
- Application connections, read/write smoke tests and background jobs work.
- The owning team checks application errors, performance and alerts.

A supported, successful in-place upgrade retains the server and its databases.
Rendering the expected manifest alone is not evidence that an upgrade completed.

## Troubleshooting and recovery

| Symptom | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Kustomize cannot find the existing patch target | Check that the team patch's API, kind, name and namespace match the shared resource. |
| The rendered engine version is wrong | Check `spec.version` in the team patch and whether the environment Kustomization references it. If the field is omitted, the shared default is inherited. |
| Kubernetes does not recognise the target API | Ask Platform Operations to check the deployed ASO release and served CRDs. |
| Azure upgrade or ASO reconciliation fails | Capture resource conditions and the Azure error. Resolve the reported prerequisite; do not delete and recreate the server to clear the error. |
| The application fails after an upgrade | Escalate with application errors and the running database version. Use the agreed recovery plan, not a version decrement in Git. |

A completed major-version upgrade cannot be downgraded in place. Recovery may
require restoring a pre-upgrade backup to a separate server and coordinating an
application cutover with Platform Operations.

